WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.301 --> 00:00:05.880
[Music] 

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:12.120
[Music]&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:19.440
Welcome, Mrs. Kalniete. You're leading Parliament's work on disinformation&nbsp;
and foreign interference on European democracy. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:26.340
I wanted to focus today more on the disinformation&nbsp;
part of your work. You have already prepared two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:31.560
sets of recommendations for the European&nbsp;
Union on how to deal with this matter and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:38.820
I understand that in this process you met with&nbsp;
many independent journalists, legislators from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:44.640
foreign countries, UN representatives...&nbsp;
What did you learn from this meetings?&nbsp;

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:49.920
Is there a pattern or specific challenges&nbsp;
in Europe when it comes to disinformation?

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:57.540
We have learned a lot from them but the&nbsp;
the general observation what I can make is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:06.420
not very optimistic because &nbsp;
the digital reality develops so fast 

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:13.860
that the legislative process is lagging&nbsp;
behind. Because technology goes faster than the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:21.480
legislative process? Yes. As one of the experts said, the&nbsp;
situation that we are discussing today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:28.560
is already yesterday. And we cannot preempt
what's going to happen... Absolutely. 

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:39.920
Secondly, what is very&nbsp;difficult
for democratic countries - and we&nbsp;are democratic countries -

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:47.640
is that we are very open&nbsp;
in our system. And those third countries who 

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:57.420
have malign interests, they are manipulating
our freedoms to intervene with our freedom.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:03.180
We can still learn from the past... We see in&nbsp;
America that disinformation has helped 

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:08.280
to fuel violence. I still remember&nbsp;
the scenes of people storming the Capitol in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:13.080
the United States and, a year after, people&nbsp;
storming the Congress in Brazil as well. 

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:17.720
Do you see a risk of this sort of violence 
also happening&nbsp;in Europe? &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:23.720
First of all I have to say that
I'm quite optimistic about Europe in that sense.

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:28.840
You are speaking 
about the unbelievable&nbsp;events 

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:36.960
in the U.S Congress or in Brazil. That&nbsp;
was the consequence of a quite long time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:49.440
of spreading different harmful information, which&nbsp;
raises tension in society and divides

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:56.340
society. And part of that lies with the&nbsp;
president of the United States. I should say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:06.780
in Europe maybe that because of the union we are&nbsp;
trained more to be inclined to a compromise culture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:16.920
and our politicians are more responsible. I&nbsp;
can't name any politician who would appeal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:27.000
to a nation just to attack the Parliament. But also why&nbsp;
I'm so optimistic is because we really understand the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:33.120
challenge of the problem and we are trying to&nbsp;
adopt legislation. We have a Digital Service&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:41.820
Act which is dealing with illegal content and&nbsp;
which puts also the responsibility on platforms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:52.260
to assess the risks and also to control the&nbsp;
content which is distributed in the social&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.260 --> 00:04:02.820
networks. Secondly, we strengthened the&nbsp;
first attempt to propose for the platforms a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:10.800
sort of agreement of behavior, which is called&nbsp;
the Democracy Action Plan. The first attempt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:21.180
was called the Code of Conduct, but it's not flawless&nbsp;
because it's voluntarily. The biggest platforms all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:27.540
agreed and they are trying to implement&nbsp;it.
But there are a multitude of small &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:36.420
servers and platforms that are not part of that&nbsp;
agreement. And some of them are quite aggressive.

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:45.900
So we have to find a way to also impose this behavior on&nbsp;
them. In the two reports that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:51.180
you prepare for Parliament you mention that&nbsp;
social network platforms are not doing their part, &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:56.700
are not doing enough. And I want to highlight&nbsp;
that your two reports, one was last year and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:02.100
other one has just been adopted this week. Does it mean&nbsp;
that in 12 months nothing has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:11.940
improved? That is the question about&nbsp;
strengthening the toolbox, which we are already

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:21.180
doing. And the most important thing is to understand how the algorithms

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:28.800
work. They have to be more transparent.&nbsp;
There has to be accessibility for

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:38.460
researchers and also for fact checkers, social&nbsp;
networks. I think the most important tool, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:49.920
Parliament already adopted, is the Digital Service&nbsp;
Act, because it's dealing with illegal content&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:57.000
and imposing on social platforms certain&nbsp;obligations 
that they have to follow. And one of your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:03.120
recommendations is that actions to counter disinformation should go hand in hand with actions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:09.360
to promote free speech and other fundamental rights.&nbsp;
This might seem contradictory. Could you explain to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:16.800
us how fighting against disinformation&nbsp;
helps promoting free speech and vice versa?

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:25.260
Sounds quite contradictory. But this is something&nbsp;
that I understand profoundly because of all of my experience.

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:35.460
Why do we need to counter disinformation? Because&nbsp;
disinformation appeals psychologically to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:49.320
human beings by serving what they want&nbsp;
to hear, and then they base their decisions and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:57.480
understanding of reality on this bubble in which&nbsp;
they are included because of likes they are making.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.480 --> 00:07:07.260
That is the job done by algorithms. Why do we need&nbsp;
to promote truly free speech and information?

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:21.120
Because that information is based on facts and by&nbsp;
providing the truthful picture of any of&nbsp;these

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:29.040
problems we are providing freedom of speech,
freedom of information. What you mention about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:34.260
algorithms make me think of one thing: there&nbsp;
are some psychological studies that say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:40.440
people tend to believe what they want to believe,&nbsp;
actually. And I guess that people and institutions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:46.560
interested in sowing disinformation&nbsp;
take profit from that. How&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:55.260
can we tackle this problem? This problem is as old,&nbsp;
as ancient as human nature. It's very

00:07:55.260 --> 00:08:01.020
very human nature. And the difference between disinformation a few centuries ago and disinformation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:07.200
currently is exactly the quantity of disinformation, the instruments with which it could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:15.780
be dispersed around the world and the speed with&nbsp;
which it's distributed. But as I mentioned, exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:25.500
because there is micro targeted disinformation.&nbsp;
For instance, the 2016 campaign of President Trump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:32.760
was precisely built on that. And that is the year&nbsp;
when democratic countries realised the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:40.980
danger of that intervention
and interference in democratic processes. 

00:08:40.980 --> 00:08:52.320
But the only way we can help to build&nbsp;
resilience, I think, is through education.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.320 --> 00:09:00.480
And here again we are facing a problem. We can&nbsp;
recommend and suggest, but the system of education&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:07.860
is entirely the responsibility of member states.&nbsp;
And are they doing enough on media literacy? There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:16.140
are quite many different programmes and projects&nbsp;
to help to deal with media literacy, but I still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.140 --> 00:09:27.600
think that we need more of a systematic approach and&nbsp;
to allocate more financial resources for that.&nbsp;

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:40.680
Because media literacy still has loopholes as to&nbsp;
very young people and also to the senior citizens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:51.120
who are out of these programmes and tools.&nbsp;
They are not so skillful to deal with them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.040 --> 00:10:03.900
My conviction, if we speak generally about 
this enormous, complex...  is that it is a mistake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:13.500
to deal with the digital revolution in&nbsp;
the compartments. I think the basic problem is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.500 --> 00:10:24.600
we don't have a complex approach for that. And if I'm&nbsp;
looking at the future, I think that we are facing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:36.420
complete change of reality with artificial&nbsp;
intelligence, which will require changing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:43.500
practically everything. There is also the&nbsp;
the question of definition... Who decides what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:50.820
is disinformation, what is true and what is a lie?
You have asked on your reports for the EU to step&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.820 --> 00:10:56.400
up action to come up with an international common&nbsp;
definition of disinformation, like some countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:04.380
have for hate crimes or for racism. Why is it
important to have a common definition of 

00:11:04.380 --> 00:11:14.160
disinformation? You have to have a common language. You&nbsp;
have to know what that word contains. But this is&nbsp;an

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:23.160
extremely difficult task, to find the&nbsp;
common definition for disinformation. I went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:31.740
to the UN with the delegation of our committee and&nbsp;
we discussed it in different departments and also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.740 --> 00:11:38.700
with different representatives from&nbsp;
different member states&nbsp;of the

00:11:38.700 --> 00:11:52.920
United Nations. It just showed what
the difficulties are. For countries in the&nbsp;&nbsp;European Union

00:11:52.920 --> 00:12:00.600
it's quite easy. Because we&nbsp;
have the same values... We have the same set of values,&nbsp;

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:06.320
 but these values are not&nbsp;the 
 values of the entire community 

00:12:06.320 --> 00:12:14.040
of the United Nations. There are
also countries in which democracy is...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.040 --> 00:12:22.440
let's call it controlled democracy, which is a sort&nbsp;
of manipulation of the elections. Then there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:31.080
autocracies and there are also dictatorships.
For them disinformation is an ideological tool to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.080 --> 00:12:39.000
ensure control over the country. and that's&nbsp;
why the European Union has to take the lead in that and at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:45.900
least agree on the term of disinformation&nbsp;
among ourselves. For several other difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.900 --> 00:12:56.160
problems we have been the the settlers of language,
the settlers of terminology, the settlers of trend.&nbsp;

00:12:56.160 --> 00:13:04.680
And then gradually it is adopted by other&nbsp;
institutions. In your report you also  introduce the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.680 --> 00:13:11.100
concept of "high-risk third country". What is this&nbsp;
about and which countries are you talking about?

00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:21.480
Currently all our relationships with the world outside from&nbsp;
European Union are based on the country agnostic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:28.740
approach, which means that we treat all&nbsp;
countries equally. But at the same time 

00:13:28.740 --> 00:13:38.640
we know that all those countries
are not equal&nbsp;if we use

00:13:38.640 --> 00:13:49.320
different objective criteria. I'm proposing 
that we treat&nbsp;high-risk third countries 

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:57.540
according to objective criteria. It could&nbsp;
be - and I will read them -

00:13:57.540 --> 00:14:05.940
the activity of foreign&nbsp;interference 
which is fact-checked and based on evidence,

00:14:05.940 --> 00:14:13.800
the systematic&nbsp;stealing of
intellectual property from&nbsp;the Union

00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:20.400
and its member states. One criteria that for me&nbsp;
is important is the consistent violation of human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.400 --> 00:14:30.360
rights in the country. For instance, in the case of&nbsp;
Russia, the blatant revision of the international order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.360 --> 00:14:36.240
by acts of aggression. You mentioned&nbsp;
Russia, China. Are there other countries that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:44.220
can mention already? There are. We can speak&nbsp;
about Iran...  Practically, there are a multitude&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.220 --> 00:14:53.280
of countries, but none of them can achieve&nbsp;
such a volume and diversity of interference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.280 --> 00:14:59.580
as Russia and China. So those are&nbsp;
the main ones? Yes. Coming back to the European&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.580 --> 00:15:05.160
elections, we are one year away from the European&nbsp;
elections. In your report you also mention that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.160 --> 00:15:11.160
Russia is investing massively in financing&nbsp;
political parties in democratic countries.

00:15:11.160 --> 00:15:18.480
How can this affect the the information scenario in&nbsp;
Europe? You are touching one of the issues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:28.920
which are absolutely vital for the existence of&nbsp;
our democracy, because democracy is an open system.&nbsp;

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:37.740
As soon as there is a hostile third&nbsp;
country coming in with its own interests,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.740 --> 00:15:48.060
financing so-called governmental, non-governmental&nbsp;
organisations or proxy foundations which are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.060 --> 00:15:57.120
used to strengthen their interests, which are&nbsp;
used to divide our societies, then we have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.120 --> 00:16:07.320
protect ourselves. I think that one of the most&nbsp;
important parts of elections is to strengthen the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.320 --> 00:16:17.280
electoral instruments, which is the voting system,&nbsp;
the counting of votes, etc. Secondly, it's a

00:16:17.280 --> 00:16:26.160
media freedom pact that provides independence&nbsp;
for media, which is protected against manipulative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:34.080
media, which supports investigative journalism.
Because investigative journalists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.080 --> 00:16:43.980
are those who can follow that dirty money, how&nbsp;
it is, in a very covert way, seeping into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.980 --> 00:16:53.160
Union and complicating the normal democratic&nbsp;
process. Of course, as we before we were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.160 --> 00:17:05.520
speaking about media literacy, about fact checking,&nbsp;
about resilience against the manipulation and fake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.520 --> 00:17:13.200
news, all of that is also important. You also alerted that&nbsp;
Russia and perhaps other countries are likely to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.200 --> 00:17:18.900
step up their misleading narratives against&nbsp;
Europe ahead of the elections to try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.900 --> 00:17:24.000
and influenciate the European elections from&nbsp;
next year. And there are some media reports that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:31.260
Russia has tripled the budget for&nbsp;
propaganda about the Ukrainian war and that part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.260 --> 00:17:35.760
of this money and this propaganda are also to be&nbsp;
spread in other countries, not only internally in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.760 --> 00:17:44.520
Russia. How can Europe oppose this&nbsp;
communication warfare? The first steps were very 

00:17:46.080 --> 00:17:57.660
speedy. We closed Sputnik and Russia Today. These
were broadcasting in many countries and in many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.660 --> 00:18:05.940
languages. That was a very strong&nbsp;
signal to Russia that we are not going to accept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.020 --> 00:18:16.500
that sort of disinformation instruments in&nbsp;
the European Union. The problem is that Russia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.500 --> 00:18:24.060
Today, Sputnik and other media immediately&nbsp;
switched to areas where we have no power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.840 --> 00:18:33.240
to forbid: to social media networks. So people&nbsp;
could still go around and watch them... Yes. Telegram, Tick Tock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.240 --> 00:18:44.760
and Facebook... Whatever! That is one of the the&nbsp;
problems. We also have to replace it with proactive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:44.760 --> 00:18:54.600
content which is attractive and interesting&nbsp;
for those who are the consumers, who have been&nbsp;the

00:18:54.600 --> 00:19:01.860
consumers of Russia Today and Sputnik. And this&nbsp;
is something that we are currently trying to do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.860 --> 00:19:06.000
To conclude this interview... We're having&nbsp;
this conversation here in the House of European&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:11.700
History, which is a museum managed by the European&nbsp;
Parliament here in Brussels. What is history's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.700 --> 00:19:18.000
role in disinformation? Does it help to counter
disinformation or does it help promote it?

00:19:18.000 --> 00:19:28.260
How&nbsp;do you see it? I would say that for years and years&nbsp;
we were quite indifferent to the historical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.260 --> 00:19:38.520
truth and to historical justice, and it created&nbsp;
a very beneficial platform for the Kremlin, for Putin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.520 --> 00:19:49.560
to manipulate historical facts, to introduce&nbsp;
their own interpretations of them. And look where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:49.560 --> 00:19:59.640
we are now. Because the Kremlin justifies their &nbsp;
aggressive, non-provoked attack on Ukraine

00:20:00.240 --> 00:20:10.440
with a complete historical manipulation&nbsp;
of facts, stating that Ukraine never has been&nbsp;an

00:20:10.440 --> 00:20:18.960
independent country, that it is not a nation.&nbsp;
It shows how important it is that we are&nbsp;

00:20:18.960 --> 00:20:27.120
scrupulous when we are dealing with history&nbsp;
and historical facts. For instance, deportations.

00:20:27.120 --> 00:20:40.560
I was born in Siberia from deported parents and in&nbsp;
this Museum I presented as a gift a letter of

00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:49.560
my grandfather from the Gulag. I would say, until our countries entered the European Union,

00:20:51.180 --> 00:21:00.960
Communism and the crimes of Communism were not&nbsp;
really on the agenda of Western countries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:00.960 --> 00:21:07.980
But, for instance, next month in the European&nbsp;
Parliament we will have for the first time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.700 --> 00:21:18.360
the official commemoration ceremony&nbsp;
for deportations of 1941. Now,

00:21:19.620 --> 00:21:30.420
30 years after the Iron Curtain fell, it's the first&nbsp;
time that a ceremony will take place. Why am I

00:21:30.420 --> 00:21:37.620
speaking about it? Because from the occupied&nbsp;
territories of Ukraine, more than 20&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:37.620 --> 00:21:45.420
000 children were deported. The Russian Duma adopted a law&nbsp;
which allows the urgent adoption of Ukrainian children.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:45.420 --> 00:21:58.560
They are deprived of their national identity,&nbsp;
 converted into someone they are&nbsp;not.

00:21:58.560 --> 00:22:07.920
It just shows that history repeats itself&nbsp;
if we do not strictly stand for truth. We accept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:07.920 --> 00:22:13.800
silence because this is more comfortable, but&nbsp;
then it's very easy to repeat it again.

00:22:13.800 --> 00:22:14.412
[Music]

